WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.648 --> 00:00:04.168
This is developer-os: long-term memory for Claude Code,

2
00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:06.600
and the discipline to actually use it.

3
00:00:07.568 --> 00:00:10.084
Every Claude Code session starts from zero.

4
00:00:10.369 --> 00:00:14.729
On Monday, you explain why webhooks are deduplicated by event ID.

5
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.091
On Tuesday, you explain it again.

6
00:00:17.321 --> 00:00:18.679
And on Wednesday…

7
00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:19.159
again.

8
00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:23.876
Decisions, lessons, and the reasons behind them simply evaporate.

9
00:00:24.303 --> 00:00:28.286
developer-os gives the agent a long-term memory that you control.

10
00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:34.786
developer-os plugs into Claude Code as a set of rules, skills, agents, and hooks.

11
00:00:35.169 --> 00:00:39.785
Next to it sits your brain: an Obsidian vault of plain Markdown files.

12
00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:44.594
Before it starts work, the agent searches the vault, and cites the notes it used.

13
00:00:44.994 --> 00:00:48.346
New knowledge goes in only as drafts, into the inbox.

14
00:00:49.795 --> 00:00:53.825
You review those drafts, and only what you approve becomes a real note.

15
00:00:56.302 --> 00:01:00.932
Code facts, plans, and ADRs stay in the repository, next to the code.

16
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:08.623
Inside, it's all plain files: one core rules file, loaded into every session.

17
00:01:08.623 --> 00:01:10.076
Thirty-four skills.

18
00:01:10.076 --> 00:01:11.988
Eleven specialist agents.

19
00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:14.206
And eight small Python hooks.

20
00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:17.265
No server, no database, nothing to host.

21
00:01:17.721 --> 00:01:21.544
During a session, developer-os steps in at five moments.

22
00:01:21.904 --> 00:01:24.359
When a session starts, it loads the core rules,

23
00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:26.971
and a list of your project's notes from the vault.

24
00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:31.329
Before a risky command, like a force push or a dependency install,

25
00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:33.497
the guard asks for your confirmation.

26
00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:37.856
When an edit adds a risky pattern, like shell=True,

27
00:01:37.856 --> 00:01:40.275
the agent hears about it right away.

28
00:01:41.415 --> 00:01:45.213
When the same call fails three times, it's told to stop retrying,

29
00:01:45.213 --> 00:01:46.323
and find the cause.

30
00:01:48.424 --> 00:01:51.442
And after a long session, it suggests a proper wrap-up.

31
00:01:54.679 --> 00:01:55.495
Hooks ask.

32
00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:56.881
They never block.

33
00:01:59.005 --> 00:02:00.468
This is the knowledge loop.

34
00:02:00.911 --> 00:02:02.297
You work in a session.

35
00:02:02.712 --> 00:02:06.872
When something is worth keeping, brain-capture writes a draft note to the inbox.

36
00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:12.076
Secrets are redacted, and the draft expires in thirty days if nobody reviews it.

37
00:02:13.812 --> 00:02:17.673
Then you run brain-review: promote, merge, or reject, but only the notes you name.

38
00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:19.486
A promoted note becomes active.

39
00:02:22.813 --> 00:02:29.411
In the next session, the agent finds it, and reads it before touching that topic.

40
00:02:31.981 --> 00:02:36.858
And once a month, brain-garden flags stale notes, duplicates, and broken links.

41
00:02:38.636 --> 00:02:41.138
Day to day, the workflow looks like this.

42
00:02:41.506 --> 00:02:44.883
spec turns an idea into a specification and a plan.

43
00:02:45.338 --> 00:02:48.525
implement-backlog builds it in small, checked batches.

44
00:02:48.906 --> 00:02:51.719
code-review rates findings from P0 to P3,

45
00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:55.837
and every serious one is double-checked by a fresh verifier.

46
00:02:56.223 --> 00:03:02.028
fix-pr applies the fixes you approve, and pr-finish waits for green CI, merges,

47
00:03:02.028 --> 00:03:03.056
and cleans up.

48
00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:06.692
At the end of the session, session-retro tells you what's done,

49
00:03:06.692 --> 00:03:07.952
and what's worth keeping.

50
00:03:08.322 --> 00:03:10.321
Every week, review the inbox.

51
00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.445
Every month, garden the vault.

52
00:03:21.535 --> 00:03:26.764
Altogether, thirty-four skills: memory, planning, building, review and shipping,

53
00:03:26.764 --> 00:03:32.059
repository health, and conventions that load on their own, when they're relevant.

54
00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:37.219
So, what do you get?

55
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.787
Context from minute one, instead of starting from zero.

56
00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:45.180
Knowledge you control: nothing becomes a fact until you approve it.

57
00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:48.274
Guardrails that ask before anything risky.

58
00:03:49.559 --> 00:03:51.448
Reviews backed by evidence.

59
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.195
And nothing to host: just Markdown, and a few Python scripts.

60
00:04:02.323 --> 00:04:04.336
Install it with two commands.

61
00:04:04.336 --> 00:04:05.586
The agent forgets.

62
00:04:05.586 --> 00:04:06.906
Your vault doesn't.

